        "contenuto_esteso": "Advertisement US, Israel war on Iran World Middle East Israel’s Netanyahu made ‘secret’ visit to UAE during Iran war The PM’s meeting with the Emirati president marks a ‘historic breakthrough’ in relations between the two countries 2 -MIN READ 2 -MIN Listen Reuters Published: 2:30am, 14 May 2026 Updated: 2:37am, 14 May 2026 Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu’s office said on Wednesday that he secretly visited the United Arab Emirates during the Iran war and spoke to the Emirati president, in what would be their first confirmed meeting. According to the statement, the meeting resulted in a “historic breakthrough” in relations between ‌Israel and the UAE. A source familiar with the meeting said Netanyahu and Sheikh Mohammed bin Zayed (MBZ) met in Al-Ain, an oasis city by the Oman border, on March 26 and that their meeting lasted several hours. Advertisement The source said that Mossad Chief Dedi Barnea made at least two visits to the UAE during ⁠the war with Iran to coordinate military actions. The intelligence chief’s visit was first reported by ‌The Wall Street Journal. Especially after coming under attack during the Iran war, the UAE has strengthened its relationships with the United States ‌and Israel, with which it opened ties in the 2020 Abraham Accords. It ⁠views the relationship ⁠with Israel as a lever for regional influence and a unique channel to Washington. Israel sent batteries for its ‌Iron Dome interception system and personnel to operate them to the UAE during the war, US Ambassador to Israel ‌Mike Huckabee ‌said on Tuesday. Advertisement Select Voice Select Speed 00:00 00:00 1.00"
